# Instruction tests that do not pass with the core-only runner on macOS.
# Used by Scripts/macos_asm_tests.py in addition to upstream's lists.
#
# Background: macOS on Apple silicon reserves the first 4GB of every 64-bit process, so
# the tests' fixed low addresses are moved up by 1TB before assembly, and the loader
# places the test's code at 0x100_0001_0000 instead of 0x1_0000. Guest addresses are host
# addresses, so nothing can be mapped where an address cut to 32 bits lands.
#
# Every entry below was checked on an M4 by running it: the runner prints the faulting
# address ("Unhandled host fault: ... at 0x..."), and each address is the moved address
# cut to 32 bits, exactly as stated. None is a translator fault. results.tsv repeats the
# reason next to each failure.

# A 32-bit address-size override (0x67) cuts the address to 32 bits; the result is below
# 4GB and cannot be mapped. Faults seen: a32_lods and a32_rep_lods at 0x80000 (RSI
# 0x1_0008_0000 cut, as the test intends); A6_addrmod and A6_REP_addrmod at 0x10000200
# (RDI 0x61626364_10000200 cut to EDI); AE_addrmod at 0x10000100 (EDI).
Test_FEX_bugs/a32_lods.asm
Test_FEX_bugs/a32_rep_lods.asm
Test_Primary/Primary_A6_REP_addrmod.asm
Test_Primary/Primary_A6_addrmod.asm
Test_Primary/Primary_AE_addrmod.asm

# The same, in the middle of a test of vector moves: `vmovd xmm2, [edx]` with RDX
# 0x100_e000_0000 faults at 0xe0000000.
Test_VEX/vmovq_vmovd_reg.asm

# Far jumps and calls through an m16:32 pointer: the test stores `lea edi, [rel .success]`,
# the code address cut to 32 bits, as the target. The translator reads a 32-bit offset
# (also for the o64 forms, as AMD does), so control goes to 0x10028, the original code
# address of .success, and faults there. The o32 forms fault first, at 0xe0000ff0,
# reading the pointer through `[esp]`.
Test_PrimaryGroup/5_FF_05.asm
Test_PrimaryGroup/5_FF_05_03_o32.asm
Test_PrimaryGroup/5_FF_05_03_o32_imm.asm
Test_PrimaryGroup/5_FF_05_03_o64.asm
Test_PrimaryGroup/5_FF_05_03_o64_imm.asm

